#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096629 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d01d6ed-1c25-5170-a1ad-7a4fa0103db7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Right Riemann Sum in Sigma Notation"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d0dd25ca-44a0-5275-b710-e7c2a70a0387" :question/math-academy-id "q-116627" :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=(x+1)^{3}$ between $x=0$ and $x=4$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?" :question/worked-solution "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're told that there are $n$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=0$ and $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-0}{n}=\\frac{4}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[f(0+j\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\frac{4}{n}{(\\frac{4j}{n}+1)}^{3}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
